&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Registration REQUIRED by 12pm on January 30, 2026 in order to attend this event.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Please join the Ukrainian Studies Program at the Harriman Institute and the Global Coalition of Ukrainian Studies (GCUS) for a lecture by &lt;strong&gt;Alim Aliev&lt;/strong&gt;. Moderated by &lt;a href="https://harriman.columbia.edu/person/yuri-shevchuk/"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Yuri Shevchuk&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This public discussion will examine the profound changes that have taken place in Crimea over the 12 years of its temporary occupation by the Russian Federation. The conversation will focus on how local residents continue to resist the occupation, how Crimean Tatars preserve and develop their identity under conditions of repression, and how they envision their future. Particular attention will be paid to human rights violations, cultural resilience, and the role of Crimea and Crimean Tatars in Ukraine&amp;rsquo;s broader struggle for freedom and independence.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Please email&amp;nbsp;disability@columbia.edu&amp;nbsp;to request disability accommodations. Advance notice is necessary to arrange for some accessibility needs.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Cover-Up: Screening and Discussion&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;He&amp;#39;s devoted his career to uncovering stories the powerful want buried. Dig into the life&amp;#39;s work of journalist Seymour Hersh.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9CxEnECKs9U" target="_blank"&gt;Cover-Up&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;is a political thriller that traces the explosive career of Pulitzer Prize-winning investigative reporter Seymour Hersh. Urgent and deeply reported, Cover-Up is both a portrait of a relentless journalist and an indictment of institutional violence &amp;mdash; revealing a cycle of impunity in the U.S. military and intelligence agencies. Drawing on exclusive access to Hersh&amp;rsquo;s notes, and interweaving primary documents and archival footage, Cover-Up captures the power and process of investigative journalism.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The screening will be followed by a conversation with&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Seymour Hersh&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;, Reporter, and&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Laura Poitras&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;, Director, Producer and Editor, moderated by&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Azmat Khan&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;, Director, Simon and June Li Center for Global Journalism.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Monday, February 2, 2026, 6:00pm - 8:30pm&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Registration required: &lt;a href="https://www.eventbrite.com/e/1980466603925?aff=oddtdtcreator" target="_blank"&gt;https://www.eventbrite.com/e/1980466603925?aff=oddtdtcreator&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This event is co-sponsored by the&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://journalism.columbia.edu/li-center" target="_blank"&gt;Simon and June Li Center for Global Journalism&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;and the&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://journalism.columbia.edu/ms-investigative-specialization" target="_blank"&gt;Toni Stabile Center for Investigative Journalism&lt;/a&gt;&amp;nbsp;at Columbia University, the&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://freedom.press/" target="_blank"&gt;Freedom of the Press Foundation&lt;/a&gt;,&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://www.ire.org/" target="_blank"&gt;Investigative Reporters and Editors&lt;/a&gt;, and the&amp;nbsp;&lt;a href="https://opcofamerica.org/" target="_blank"&gt;Overseas Press Club&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Please join the Herbert Lehman Center for American History and the Institute for Research in African-American Studies for a discussion with Brian Kwoba (University of Memphis) of his&amp;nbsp;biography&amp;nbsp;of Hubert H. Harrison, a long-forgotten founder of Black radical thought. The conversation will be moderated by Brent Hayes Edwards.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr /&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The significance of Hubert Henry Harrison (1883&amp;ndash;1927)&amp;mdash;as a journalist, activist, and educator&amp;mdash;lies in his innovation of radical solutions to radical injustices.&amp;nbsp;He did far more than cultivate the rich, dark soil in which the so-called &amp;ldquo;Harlem Renaissance&amp;rdquo; would take root. Harrison also played a pivotal role in the rise of Marcus Garvey and the emergence of the largest international organization of African people in modern history. Because of his fearless radicalism, however, he has been erased from popular memory. Brian Kwoba&amp;#39;s biography draws deeply on his research in&amp;nbsp;the &lt;a href="https://dlc.library.columbia.edu/hubert_h_harrison" target="_blank"&gt;Hubert Harrison Papers&lt;/a&gt;, which are held at the Rare Book &amp;amp; Manuscript Library.&amp;nbsp;&lt;br /&gt;
&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;ldquo;Brian Kwoba has written a beautiful, intellectual biography as radical and original as its subject. He excavates Hubert H. Harrison&amp;mdash;brilliant Marxist, Black nationalist, internationalist, and gender rebel&amp;mdash;revealing dimensions even his most scrupulous chroniclers missed.&amp;rdquo;&amp;mdash;Robin D. G. Kelley, author of&amp;nbsp;&lt;em&gt;Freedom Dreams: The Black Radical Imagination&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Registration REQUIRED by 4pm on February 2, 2026 in order to attend this event.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Please join the Harriman Institute and the Saltzman Institute of War and Peace Studies for a conversation with&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;Alexandra Vacroux&lt;/strong&gt; and&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;Yevhen Malik&lt;/strong&gt;.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Join us for an in-depth conversation on how wars end&amp;mdash;and what the war termination literature can tell us about possible pathways for ending Russia&amp;rsquo;s war against Ukraine. Drawing on both scholarly analysis and lived experience, the discussion will examine historical patterns of war termination, the conditions under which negotiations succeed or fail, and how these insights apply to the current war.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The event brings together Yevhen Malik, a veteran of the Armed Forces of Ukraine with extensive frontline and leadership experience, including prolonged captivity in Russia, and Dr. Alexandra Vacroux, former executive director of the Davis Center at Harvard University and now vice president for strategic engagement at the Kyiv School of Economics. Together, they will assess the current state of negotiations among Ukraine, Russia, and the United States, and reflect on the political, military, and human factors shaping prospects for an end to the war.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Please email&amp;nbsp;disability@columbia.edu&amp;nbsp;to request disability accommodations. Advance notice is necessary to arrange for some accessibility needs.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Join ERM program Part-Time Lecturer Melody Feinberg as she interviews Ron Cathcart, Vice Chair of the Natixis US Risk Committee and former U.S. representative to the Policy Development Group of the Basel Committee on Banking Supervision. Cathcart previously served as head of Enterprise Risk Supervision at the Federal Reserve Bank of New York and chief enterprise risk officer at Washington Mutual, with additional senior risk roles at CIBC, Bank One, and RBC.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Ron will discuss how boards can elevate oversight by embracing strategic risk dialogue, sharpening information flow, and embedding scenario analysis into decision-making. He will emphasize the importance of board composition, management alignment, and a forward-looking mindset that treats risk as a strategic capability rather than a compliance exercise. Boards must be willing to challenge management and engage deeply in discussions around risk appetite and strategic choices.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Cathcart will also explore the rising importance of scenario-based planning to anticipate disruption and the need for management to bring data-driven &amp;ldquo;what-if&amp;rdquo; analyses to the boardroom. Ron will highlight emerging risks reshaping oversight&amp;mdash;including AI, cyber threats, and geopolitical shifts&amp;mdash;and illustrate how a proactive, forward-leaning board can help shape the organization&amp;rsquo;s risk posture, resilience, and long-term success.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Speakers&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
∙&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;Ron Cathcart&lt;/strong&gt;, Vice Chair of the Natixis US Risk Committee and former U.S. representative to the Policy Development Group of the Basel Committee on Banking Supervision&lt;br /&gt;
∙&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;Melody Feinberg&lt;/strong&gt;, Independent Board Member, Axiom Bank and Security Mutual Life Insurance Company of New York; former Chief Risk Officer of Federal Home Loan Bank of New York&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
